WEBVTT

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:29.830
Let me warn you and let me warn the nation against the smooth evasion that says, of course we believe these things. We believe in science.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:40.340
security. We believe in work for the unemployed. We believe in saving homes. Cross our hearts

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:51.490
and hope to die. We believe in all these things. But we do not like the way the present administration

00:00:51.490 --> 00:01:00.000
is doing that. Just turn them over to us. We will do all

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:14.000
We will do more of them, we will do them better, and most important of all, the doing of them will not cost anybody anything.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:37.600
a much bigger thrill when they buy one. This couple is going through a model house now.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:44.260
Suppose we follow them. The husband apparently isn't very keen about it all, but you know

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:49.880
how wives are. So in they go to this delightful living room. The girl is tremendously impressed

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.080
with the room and its furnishings, but the fellow still just doesn't seem interested.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:00.000
Just give him, oh, a good book and a comfortable chair. Until his wife sees him, then it's

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.010
story. Get up out of there, boy, and get going. They find that the dining room is small, but

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:12.160
adequate for their small family and for moderate entertaining. And so they pass on to the kitchen.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:22.460
This cheerful room with its many handy cabinets impresses even this skeptic. And his wife is

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:29.300
entranced by the modern flat-top stove and by such convenient features as the sturdy built-in

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.000
ironing board.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:58.640
Well let's see what we find upstairs. First a peek into the guest room with its modern furniture. Then a glance into the bathroom with its glass dressing table. He likes this even though the water isn't turned on yet. Just a fleeting glimpse of the nursery for they don't need this room. Yet.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.570
and then a thorough inspection of the master bedroom.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.710
This canopy bed strikes his fancy,

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:10.340
and it seems quite comfortable too.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:15.130
But the wife is more interested in the ample closet space.

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:18.560
And so they leave, reluctantly it seems,

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.260
for they both would like to have this place

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.470
for their very own.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.640
Too bad they can't afford it.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.550
Ah, but maybe they can,

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:27.650
for according to this sign,

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.000
they can buy this house with monthly payments

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.050
less than they now spend for rent.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:13.870
years ago the cry was westward hoe as pioneers build a new nation. Nature was kinder, but

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:21.590
today drought and impoverished soil have forced a new exodus northward to Alaska. From these

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:27.260
barren farms in Michigan, Wisconsin, Minnesota, 1,500 men, women, and children are moving.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.000
Uncle Sam is lending the money. He's taking them to

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:36.570
pastures in Alaska to fertile land, normal climate. Here's one family ready to go.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:41.000
I hope when we get in Alaska that things will be better up there and I can make a

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.700
living without asking anyone for help. My ancestors weren't afraid to go in a new

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:52.430
country. Why should we be? When we get there I'll do my best to make a real home

00:04:52.430 --> 00:05:00.000
for my family. For years life here has been severe, but in Alaska 40 acres of land for

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.910
For each family, a $3,000 loan, farm equipment, a new chance to live.

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:11.090
It's a 3,500-mile journey, but none are happier to go than these wives and mothers

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.890
who close a cheerless door behind them for the last time.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:18.540
Pioneers of the 20th century.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:22.170
Interested?

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:26.870
Write to Harry L. Hopkins, F.E.R.A. Washington, who's doing the job for the president.

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:29.700
The Vanguard Leaves.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.000
At San Francisco, the North Star sets out with 80 skilled workers who clear the way,

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.640
build huts, carry food and tools.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:44.980
Northward ho to southern Alaska, mild climate, rich soil.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:51.210
A new caravan moves on, not this way, but with the same spirit that guided our forefathers.

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:58.200
I come from Alabama with a banjo on my knees.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.570
have seen shacks like this one.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.670
Millions of them housed whose breadwinners once had steady jobs.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.040
Now they have been out of work for years.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:40.740
Many of them face the prospect of being jobless for some time to come.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.520
Their jobs have been taken by machinery.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.010
Every time a dial system is installed,

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:48.910
it takes jobs away from two-thirds of the telephone operators.

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:51.990
When sound was introduced into motion pictures,

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.560
it forced out almost half of the nation's actors and musicians.

00:06:55.780 --> 00:07:00.000
However, the jobless may hope to escape from such shack.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:12.070
The Relief Administration is building rural communities where new industries may find workers and where these people may grow their own food in place of the direct relief paid for by the more fortunate.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:19.470
The rural communities are also workshops where the new pioneers may make the things they can't buy.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:24.290
The first of these rural communities is in the tiny woods belt of East Texas, Wood Lake.

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:35.410
Here's Wood Lake at the very beginning. These men had been on relief until they got this

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:41.440
work. You see them at the start of the job, hauling logs to the colony sawmill. At Wood

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.650
Lake, they cut only part of their lumber on the spot, but it's different at some of the

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:51.030
other rural community developments of the FERA. For instance, at colony number one in

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:56.650
Arkansas, practically all the lumber was home sawed and cost much less than it would have

00:07:56.650 --> 00:08:00.000
if bought. Here at Wood Lake, these

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:04.510
trained carpenters and painters who had been on the relief rolls played their part in the

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:09.920
construction of the houses and other buildings. Each man got the full wage scale as provided for

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:15.260
in the code governing the industry, and each man was provided with enough work hours per week to

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:21.870
keep his family and food and other necessities. These men are making hens nests, making them out

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:30.000
of the scraps left from the house building. Well, let's meet some of the colonists. These men once were white-collar

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.920
workers. Now they're farmers, doing very well indeed at the job. For instance, corn growers,

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:42.280
they rate right well. Here's a foreman inspecting their corn. He says it's plenty good for this

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:51.990
section. This is prize-winning ribbon cane, grown by the first colonists to put in a crop at Woodlake.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:59.760
Observe the community canning plant. This year, the colonists put up 42,000 cans of their homegrown tomatoes,

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:04.110
And firewood they cut the first summer, stacked up almost a thousand cords.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:10.120
Or did they forget the winter's fodder supply for the farm animals, put away plenty.

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:22.360
Some of the men came on while the colony was building and got the first crop started.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:29.040
Their pay, the way they put it, was $21.50 a week, 21 meals, and 50 cents.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.100
from daylight till dark at this sort of job in the fields. When they came in from

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:37.460
the fields they often pitched in and worked at the canning plant till midnight.

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:41.980
After that kind of a day they could eat their own cooking and like it. They could

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:46.370
sleep like babies. After four months of roughing it they were still getting along

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:50.890
with each other. They worked together on the large field in which each man has 12

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:55.190
acres. He also has a garden of three acres near his home.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:10:00.000
Colonists come back from the fields and put up the cooperatively owned

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.940
mules for the night. By the way, they buy these mules with funds supplied by the

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:09.480
self-help division of the relief administration. Naturally, the

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:14.000
administration's first step must be to help the families going on the land to get

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:21.810
work animals. This shed at Wood Lake is full of farm implements, also owned and worked

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:26.800
cooperatively. Here's cooperation in another part of farming, keeping up the

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.000
fertility of the soil. No barnyard manure goes to

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.020
waste at Woodlake. The East Texas sandy loam, like any other soil, must have its

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:40.380
fertility replenished. So the farm manager makes applying this fertilizer just as

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.560
much a part of the Woodlake farming job as plowing or harrowing.

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:49.230
Observe this building closely. It may turn out to be the most important

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:53.700
structure in the community, the shop. Perhaps from it will grow up little

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:58.450
industries to give the colonists cash income and make them consumers once again.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:07.630
let's look in on a Woodlake arrival. Truck is bringing down the road a man who was once

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:12.460
a public accountant in Houston but who lost his job and had been on the relief rolls for

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:18.530
a year until Woodlake came along. He came up several months ago and batched it while

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:23.400
his family stayed in Houston. Now he knows what it's like here and is bringing up the

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:30.000
folks. While they roll down the road we can see some of the houses in Woodlake. You notice

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.700
lines the first settlers in East Texas followed in designing their homes. Most of

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:38.220
them have the open porch in the center in order to let the gulf breezes flow

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:43.390
through. That's sensible. Most native architecture is sensible. That's why the

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:47.340
relief administration follows it in planning each rural community. There's

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:51.980
another reason. Following the native style of architecture, these houses have

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:55.500
been made beautiful at less than it would have cost to make them ugly.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:12:00.000
Why is that? Well, it's because the native styles follow

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.950
simple natural lines. They were developed by men whose first thought was to make houses

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:09.310
that would be easy to build and comfortable under the climatic conditions prevailing in

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:15.440
their section. A bird's eye view, which certainly shows that these houses are far apart so the

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:22.020
colonists know they're out in the wide open spaces, 300 feet between houses. Oops, almost

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:30.000
forgot our stranded public accountant from Houston. Well, here he is, turning into the

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.340
as the truck goes by.

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:34.200
Well, it looks like home sweet home

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:36.480
with ground and lofty improvements to the kids.

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:39.250
The father has leased this place for three years.

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:41.790
He has the option of buying it the third year.

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.260
He can pay it out at $15 a month.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:45.700
Where will he get the money?

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:47.750
Why, from his share of the proceeds

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:49.270
from the surplus farm products

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:50.790
over and above their own needs,

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:53.410
the colonists deliver to the Relief Administration.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:56.920
That is a pretty light unloading job, isn't it?

00:12:57.340 --> 00:12:59.760
But that's all the furniture the family had left

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.180
after Dad was out of work for a year.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:06.440
While we're down this bay, we'll drop in on one of the neighbors.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:09.280
The colony's social worker is just ahead of us,

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:12.680
so we'll be on our way and not disturb her important work.

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:16.230
Part of her job is to help the mothers manage the family diet

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.310
to protect the health of the kids.

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:21.440
More health protection, good sanitation,

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:24.130
porcelain and bathtubs, and all that goes with them.

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:26.350
Want to know where the water comes from?

00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:28.240
All right, we know all the answers.

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:29.830
Here's the Community Water Works.

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:35.010
Each family has a poultry house like this.

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:43.800
The surplus chickens and eggs go to the Relief Administration as part payment on indebtedness.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:47.570
Now forget carking care.

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:49.860
There's a place for play in Wood Lake,

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:54.010
a park of almost 300 acres given to the colony by a Texas woman.

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:59.090
It contains two lakes, and there's eight-pound bass to be caught here, so they say,

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.140
peaking day in the park. Plenty of room for everyone, and stone ovens and barbecue pits

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:10.850
and split log tables were on to spread your vines, or if you prefer, your vettles. While

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:14.550
mother spreads the table, the kids can get acquainted with the fair face of dame nature.

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:19.320
That's one of the finest things about these rural communities, the opportunity they give

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:24.620
the kids to enjoy the great out-of-doors. The groves, you know, were man's first temple.

00:14:24.620 --> 00:14:30.000
The darkling wood, amidst the cool and silence, man offered to the mightiest his solemn

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.580
thanks. Thus before the days of history, and thus today. And the minister's grace expresses the

00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:41.890
thanks of these people for the chance once again to enjoy such a picnic. The enjoyment starts with

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:48.460
the food. Can the colonists take it? The fellowship that follows the food is important too. When people

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:54.780
play together, it's easier for them to work together. After a good meal, it's easier to take

00:14:54.780 --> 00:15:00.000
a speech. This one isn't hard to take. The colony administrator is telling the story of his

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.660
other experiences with similar groups. There's provision at Woodlake for fun

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:08.800
indoors as well as out. This community house was given with the park.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:12.910
Here the people have their public gatherings both for pleasure and business,

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:17.260
and it's a musical center, this community house. Colonists have their own orchestra,

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:21.380
also a quartet and choral organization. The Woodlake

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:25.890
Church, it's non-sectarian. Each denomination in turn invites

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:30.000
a representative to hold services, and the congregation is of good sight,

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:41.710
no matter what the faith of the leader for any particular service. The New Deal has given

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:47.160
these people what they wanted more than anything else in the round world: a chance to exchange

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:54.780
their work for home, food, clothing, and education for their children. Contrast a lot of the

00:15:54.780 --> 00:16:00.000
shack-housed family with the contentment of this Woodlake group. The families in the colonies

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:05.140
path of the pioneer once again they're taking a fresh start on the new frontier

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:22.600
my wife is sitting next to me and on her knee is Anna Eleanor Dahl our oldest

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:29.760
grandchild and then comes my daughter Anna Dahl and on her lap

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:40.190
fuzzy. And then comes my mother standing behind me, Franklin Jr., and my oldest boy, James,

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:47.570
and my youngest boy, John, and my son-in-law, Curtis Dow. So we're starting on the cruise

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:50.960
tomorrow. What's our campaign slogan, Sissy?

00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:53.700
Happy days are here again.

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:00.740
Yeah.

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:15.860
begins it's from dawn to dusk and after that's the way things are on the farm

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:23.730
summer and winter go by it may be dark in the barn but you've got the milk just

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:27.970
the same it may be hot or cold but the feed has to go to the cattle

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:44.300
A farmer works at a dozen trades through the day.

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:47.530
And so does his wife and his sons and his daughters.

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:50.900
For Bill Parkinson, a long day's work has begun.

00:20:06.170 --> 00:20:10.460
everybody has to help if you marry a farmer that's the first thing you learn

00:20:10.460 --> 00:20:23.200
goodbye but the water has to be pumped and the fire lighted this is 1940 but the farm woman's

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:30.000
day is long they don't complain women like hazel parkinson but they know on an august 9th

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:32.530
how hot the stove was going to be at noon.

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:35.060
They may not say much about it,

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:38.520
but they wish you could just turn a faucet to get your water

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:40.200
the way you can in town.

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:44.130
They know, and their children know,

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:47.030
the work that goes into raising food for a nation.

00:21:31.660 --> 00:21:59.730
Ringing in the pail.

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:05.750
and clean fresh milk for the town the city milk that makes bones and muscles

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:11.060
for the children of the nation but milk has to be properly cooled or it'll spoil

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:22.960
and sour and that takes back an arm milk is the cash crop for a family of

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:27.900
seven here on the Parkinson farm the check from the dairy means sugar and

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:29.920
coffee clothes and shoes

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:39.730
and all the things this earth won't grow.

00:22:39.730 --> 00:23:00.000
It's good land here, but good.

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:09.020
long-settled land. Bill Parkinson was born here in 1889, a year his father bought the farm. Bill's

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:16.920
carried it on, improved it, changed the changing conditions, 205 acres. He used to do general

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:23.240
farming, but the land got tired. Now Bill and his sons cultivate about 30 acres.

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:30.000
He rotates corn and oats, red clover and grass and alfalfa. Not the best land,

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:36.280
Not the worst, but it has raised five healthy Americans and Bill and Hazel.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:38.420
That's something for land to do.

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:06.630
The milk cook for the dairy.

00:24:39.370 --> 00:25:00.000
This hot day, there's something wrong.

00:25:08.510 --> 00:25:11.750
Sour milk, good for pigs, but not for human beings.

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:16.870
Hard to cool milk right in August, if you haven't the right sort of pump or equipment.

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:21.580
Milk today must pass modern standards, dairy standards.

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:44.400
Sour milk, good for pigs, but the milk check won't be so big this month.

00:26:35.630 --> 00:26:41.630
8 o'clock, time for Tom and Dan to get the alfalfa in while their father goes to Cornfield.

00:26:41.630 --> 00:26:45.910
Cooperation? On a farm that's just horse sense.

00:26:45.910 --> 00:26:49.020
A farm is the oldest cooperative in the world.

00:26:49.020 --> 00:26:53.270
It stays a farm, but it takes on new skills and new ways.

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:09.730
wash day in the women's work. Seven people make a big wash, but it's got to be done every week.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:16.490
You can't leave your men folks dirty, they might get used to it. Heat the water, carry the water,

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:22.220
pour it in the tub every week, just like cleaning the lamps. That's the old way,

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:29.800
the hard way. Yes, there is a machine to do the wash, but it runs by electricity.

00:28:30.570 --> 00:28:36.720
There are lamps you don't have to clean and trim and fill, but they run by electricity.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:41.990
Here on the farm where it's needed most, electricity is hard to get.

00:28:41.990 --> 00:28:44.290
Power companies want to profit.

00:28:44.290 --> 00:28:49.840
They get it in the city where people are scrunched up together, but the farms are left in the

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:51.140
dark.

00:28:51.140 --> 00:29:00.000
Three farms out of four are left in the dark, 75% of all farms in this big inventive country.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:01.320
She's wrong somehow.

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:40.470
Pitch it up and load it right.

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:45.270
We're getting the hay in.

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:49.750
The hay that means feed and good milk and cows chewing in the barn.

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:54.100
We're taking something from the Earth and we'll give it back to the Earth.

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:58.000
And in between, it will feed American cattle and American kids.

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:04.410
The load's part of us, and we're part of the load.

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:06.120
Pitch her up and build her steady.

