Kid is about the best crop there is.

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:58.950
is up on the line. It took till now. Only there's still the ironing to be done. Lester, yes, that's a good load.

00:33:00.160 --> 00:33:17.120
A good load, a first-class load, the shadows turning east and growing.

00:33:58.190 --> 00:34:00.000
He died 70 years ago, before he left

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:06.840
electricity was born. The lamp and stove were good tools in their day, but there are better

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:13.490
ones now. Good people, hardworking people deserve the best tools man can make.

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:30.000
Bless this food to this family. They have earned it, not by easy tasks, but with their

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:36.210
strength in their toil. They're wise in the ways of the earth. They are a united family.

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:42.680
Now they're tired at the end of the day. They're friendly with each other, glad to see each

00:34:42.680 --> 00:34:49.150
other's faces. They may not say very much, but they have the word home in their hearts.

00:34:50.530 --> 00:34:57.780
Things we cherish most in America are here at this table. While we foster and maintain

00:34:57.780 --> 00:35:00.000
them, it shall be well with all.

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:30.000
The long day ends in quietude, but the days

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:31.120
work, is it?

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:13.820
lamplight, and homework.

00:36:13.820 --> 00:36:17.460
The capital of Illinois is Springfield,

00:36:17.460 --> 00:36:20.970
and the capital of Nebraska is Lincoln.

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:23.950
But the letters and words to tired eyes

00:36:23.950 --> 00:36:26.740
blur and dance on the page.

00:36:26.740 --> 00:36:29.480
She doesn't care so much about her own eyes,

00:36:30.000 --> 00:37:00.000
her children's has to have a good edge for

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:07.380
tomorrow's morning wouldn't be so hard if you had a good light on your work wouldn't be so hard with

00:37:07.380 --> 00:37:17.300
power to turn the grindstone they found that out in the cities in the factories but one man can't

00:37:17.300 --> 00:37:18.380
change that alone

00:37:49.580 --> 00:38:00.000
The same children working to kill them

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:30.000
The knives are cutting, the load piles high, the sun beats down from the August sky. We built our freedom and

00:38:30.030 --> 00:38:37.170
this way from Mississippi to Iowa way from Oregon to the rocks of Maine we're

00:38:37.170 --> 00:38:39.220
building it still together

00:39:02.010 --> 00:39:04.870
When we get together, we're hard to stop.

00:39:04.870 --> 00:39:07.790
We can raise the crop and harvest the crop.

00:39:07.790 --> 00:39:10.550
We can get the power and get the light.

00:39:10.550 --> 00:39:13.570
We can get the things that we want today,

00:39:13.570 --> 00:39:16.620
with neighbors working the self-same way,

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:19.460
working together to cut the corn.

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:38.370
Now they talk it over, the country way, the slow, cautious decision of the people.

00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:42.990
Electricity, about time we got around here.

00:39:44.030 --> 00:39:46.670
Well, it sure would help me out, and the woman too.

00:39:47.410 --> 00:39:53.550
But the power company says, you can see their lines go cross-country, see them in the sky,

00:39:53.950 --> 00:39:56.090
but they don't bring the power down to the farm.

00:39:57.050 --> 00:40:00.000
Say it costs too much, say a lot of things.

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:12.960
"I'd sure like to light up the barn, and the house, too. Running water. Ellen's always saying if we had running water, power company won't do it."

00:40:12.960 --> 00:40:29.410
"But I hear there's a new kind of power. Government. That's right. I hear there's an agency. Rural Electrification. Well now, how do you go about it? I ain't for it, but I'm ready to be convinced."

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:31.700
I'm for it if it gets us the power.

00:40:32.560 --> 00:40:33.680
Why don't we get a meeting?

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:36.400
A meeting and hear about it, find out about it.

00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:40.060
Don't have to take it unless we want, but find out about it.

00:40:40.380 --> 00:40:42.260
Government power for our farm.

00:40:44.080 --> 00:40:48.240
One night in the district schoolhouse, Bill Parkins and neighbors get together.

00:40:49.060 --> 00:40:51.420
They listen and argue and ask questions.

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:53.040
They speak their mind.

00:40:53.460 --> 00:40:54.620
Some are not easy to convince.

00:40:55.290 --> 00:40:56.290
What's it going to cost?

00:40:56.530 --> 00:40:57.390
Who's going to run it?

00:40:57.510 --> 00:40:59.250
Why is the government doing it?

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:07.280
going to own the lines. The county agent meets them on their own ground. The REA was set up by

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:13.600
the president and the Congress in 1935 to help farmers to get the power and light at a price

00:41:13.600 --> 00:41:21.090
they can afford. The REA loans money at low interest rates for line construction and where

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:26.630
necessary for generating plants to the cooperative set up by the farmers in the community.

00:41:26.630 --> 00:41:29.380
There are no private investors, no profit-making.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:33.500
You don't risk any cash yourself except a small membership fee.

00:41:34.040 --> 00:41:35.860
You get power at cost.

00:41:36.510 --> 00:41:40.850
When the loan from the government to your cooperative is paid back through your electric bills,

00:41:41.230 --> 00:41:45.690
you and your neighbors will own your own electric system, your own lines.

00:42:05.280 --> 00:42:08.060
In 1935, R.E.A. is born.

00:42:08.060 --> 00:42:11.680
Ninety percent of all American farms without lighter power.

00:42:12.220 --> 00:42:13.180
The cities are lighted.

00:42:13.510 --> 00:42:14.970
The country is still in the dark.

00:42:15.630 --> 00:42:16.150
Lights up.

00:42:16.690 --> 00:42:17.050
1936.

00:42:18.350 --> 00:42:20.130
Twenty-six R.E.A. systems.

00:42:20.710 --> 00:42:25.280
Seven thousand five hundred farm families like Bill Parkinson's get light.

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:26.990
1937.

00:42:27.470 --> 00:42:28.170
Lights up.

00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:30.000
There are one hundred and twenty-two cities.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:37.290
systems now. Power and light for 43,000 farm families. Lights up for Mississippi and Ohio.

00:42:37.870 --> 00:42:49.780
Lights up north, south, east, west. 1939, 548 systems. 435,000 families and other users and many

00:42:49.780 --> 00:42:57.590
more soon to come on the lines. Lights up. 600 systems operating in early 1940, spreading their

00:42:57.590 --> 00:43:00.000
requires over 45 states.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:05.940
over half a million farms, schools, churches, stores, and rural industrial plants getting

00:43:05.940 --> 00:43:12.240
electric service for the first time. 300,000 more expected to come on these lines, and

00:43:12.240 --> 00:43:17.440
new lines going up almost everywhere at the rate of 500 miles a day.

00:43:33.770 --> 00:43:37.680
The city, a place that always has power.

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:46.780
Wires swing out to the country.

00:43:46.780 --> 00:43:51.770
They're stretching out, long wires reaching out where wires never went before.

00:43:51.770 --> 00:43:55.480
There's a tune as the wind blows through the wires.

00:44:05.120 --> 00:44:16.060
power for the parkinsons they're lifting a pole back in revolutionary days in this country

00:44:16.510 --> 00:44:24.000
we used to plant a pole and call it a liberty tree this pole has been a liberty tree to thousands of farm families

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:42.300
Hoist up the transformer.

00:44:43.270 --> 00:44:45.450
It means kilowatt hours of electricity.

00:44:46.390 --> 00:44:48.350
One kilowatt hour will do a week's wash.

00:44:48.650 --> 00:44:49.950
Grind 100 pounds of grain.

00:44:50.920 --> 00:44:53.160
One kilowatt hour will hoist two tons of hay.

00:44:54.030 --> 00:44:55.790
Kilowatt hours, don't get tired.

00:46:06.880 --> 00:46:11.240
Bill Parkinson is now a member of the Belmont Electric Cooperative.

00:46:35.280 --> 00:46:38.240
I didn't know a stove could look as clean as that.

00:46:38.240 --> 00:46:40.520
Looks as if it would bake good, too.

00:46:40.520 --> 00:47:00.000
Tuesday, August the 8th, 1940, southwestern Ohio. Scattered showers and

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:04.820
thunderstorms tonight and Friday. Cooler Friday afternoon and night. Much cooler in southwest

00:47:04.820 --> 00:47:11.600
portions of the state. Here's your weather forecast. The United States weather. Electricity means running

00:47:11.600 --> 00:47:18.950
water on the farm. You can throw the old pump handle away. This pump works all the time for a few cents a day.

00:48:07.160 --> 00:48:12.000
clean ice, you'll worry about things sorrowing and spoiling in summer.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:26.660
"Farmers read their own meters. That may sound queer to people in the city, but in a cooperative, if you tried to cheat, you'd only be cheating yourself."

00:49:56.840 --> 00:49:59.180
Fresh milk. Clean milk for the town.

00:50:00.220 --> 00:50:01.160
by the running water.

00:50:01.890 --> 00:50:02.990
No returns from the dairy.

00:50:03.470 --> 00:50:05.410
No more milk that should have fed the children

00:50:05.410 --> 00:50:06.350
and gone to the pigs.

00:50:13.280 --> 00:50:22.440
Fellas, we got an electric mama now.

00:50:36.170 --> 00:50:39.130
A turn of the hand means drinking water for the horses.

00:50:39.130 --> 00:50:42.100
A turn of the hand instead of minutes at the pump.

00:51:14.300 --> 00:51:17.440
A big week's wash, but it won't ache any woman's back.

00:51:17.440 --> 00:51:22.950
No bending over the scrub board, no rubbing and scrubbing while your arms and fingers ache.

00:51:43.050 --> 00:51:48.470
first thing women ask for after light itself. If you have ever ironed for an

00:51:48.470 --> 00:51:53.920
hour you'll know why. An electric iron may not look important to a man, but it

00:51:53.920 --> 00:51:56.950
lightens one whole day's burden for a woman.

00:52:15.230 --> 00:52:19.630
a lady in Missouri even taught her ringer how to shell peas.

00:52:19.630 --> 00:52:23.590
I won't ask for an affidavit on that, but she says it's true.

00:52:52.170 --> 00:52:57.850
We've got the power from the High Lines now to help us out through night and day.

00:52:57.850 --> 00:53:00.000
We've got new tools for the old toil.

00:53:00.990 --> 00:53:03.450
We'll learn their ways and set them to work.

00:53:03.450 --> 00:53:05.510
Set them to work to help us all.

00:53:05.510 --> 00:53:08.150
Neighbors working together.

00:53:08.150 --> 00:53:11.240
We hired the money and brought the wires.

00:53:11.240 --> 00:53:13.860
It's our own power and our own light,

00:53:13.860 --> 00:53:15.770
and it will belong to all of us.

00:53:15.770 --> 00:53:17.980
All of us here together.

00:53:17.980 --> 00:53:21.170
It's a friendly sound when the motor whirs.

00:53:21.170 --> 00:53:24.400
It's a friendly sight when the lights go on.

00:53:24.400 --> 00:53:28.030
It's the light and power we've never had,

00:53:28.030 --> 00:53:30.000
but we've got it now together.

00:53:45.070 --> 00:53:49.330
The long day ends, things will be easier now.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:33.680
Back to dear old Charlton far away.

00:54:33.680 --> 00:54:37.560
Since Roosevelt's been re-elected, we'll not be neglected.

00:54:37.560 --> 00:54:41.580
We've got Franklin D. Roosevelt back again.

00:54:41.580 --> 00:54:45.700
Back again, back again, back again.

00:54:45.700 --> 00:54:49.880
We've got Franklin D. Roosevelt back again.

00:54:49.880 --> 00:54:53.640
Since Roosevelt's been elected, moonshine liquor's been corrected.

00:54:53.640 --> 00:54:57.650
We've got a legal one, whiskey, beer, and gin.

00:55:02.430 --> 00:55:04.570
I've got great news, boys.

00:55:04.570 --> 00:55:05.570
What's exciting?

00:55:05.570 --> 00:55:06.570
The depression is over.

00:55:06.570 --> 00:55:07.400
The depression is over?

00:55:07.400 --> 00:55:08.400
Over, do you realize that?

00:55:08.400 --> 00:55:09.290
The factories are opening up.

00:55:09.290 --> 00:55:11.290
Men are going back to over the thousands.

00:55:11.290 --> 00:55:13.290
Our farm products are being sold the world over.

00:55:13.290 --> 00:55:14.290
Savings accounts are heaping up.

00:55:14.290 --> 00:55:16.290
The banks are pouring out new loans.

00:55:16.290 --> 00:55:17.290
There is no unemployment.

00:55:17.290 --> 00:55:18.290
Fear has been banished.

00:55:18.290 --> 00:55:19.290
Confidence reborn.

00:55:19.290 --> 00:55:21.290
Poverty has been wiped out.

00:55:21.290 --> 00:55:22.290
Laughter is out throughout the nation.

00:55:22.290 --> 00:55:24.290
The people are happy again.

00:55:24.290 --> 00:55:25.290
We're out of the race!

00:55:25.290 --> 00:55:27.140
